<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<usx version="2.6">
  <book code="EST" style="id">- Tov Rose New Messianic Version Bible</book>
  <para style="h">Ester</para>
  <para style="mt">Esther</para>
  <chapter number="1" style="c" />
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="1" style="v" />Now it came to pass in the days of Achashverosh <char style="add">[I will be silent and poor]</char>, (this <char style="add">[is]</char> Achashverosh <char style="add">[I will be silent and poor]</char> which reigned, from India even unto Ethiopia <char style="add">[Kush]</char>, <char style="add">[over]</char> an hundred and seven and twenty provinces:)</para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="2" style="v" />
    <char style="add">[That]</char> in those days, when the king Achashverosh <char style="add">[I will be silent and poor]</char> sat on the throne of his kingdom, which <char style="add">[was]</char> in Shushan <char style="add">[lily]</char> the capitol,</para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="3" style="v" />In the third year of his reign, he made a feast unto all his princes and his servants; the power of Paras <char style="pn">(Persia)</char> <char style="add">[pure or splendid]</char> and Madai <char style="add">[middle land]</char>, the nobles and princes of the provinces, <char style="add">[being]</char> before him:</para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="4" style="v" />When he showed the riches of his glorious kingdom and the honour of his excellent majesty many days, <char style="add">[even]</char> an hundred and eighty days.</para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="5" style="v" />And when these days were expired, the king made a feast unto all the people that were present in Shushan <char style="add">[lily]</char> the palace, both unto great and small, seven days, in the court of the garden of the king’s palace;</para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="6" style="v" />
    <char style="add">[Where were]</char> white, green, and blue, <char style="add">[hangings]</char>, fastened with cords of fine linen and purple to silver rings and pillars of marble: the beds <char style="add">[were of]</char> gold and silver, upon a pavement of red, and blue, and white, and black, marble.</para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="7" style="v" />And they gave <char style="add">[them]</char> drink in vessels of gold, <char style="pn">(the vessels being diverse one from another,)</char> and royal wine in abundance, according to the state of the king.</para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="8" style="v" />And the drinking <char style="add">[was]</char> according to the law; none did compel: for so the king had appointed to all the officers of his house, that they should do according to every man’s pleasure.</para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="9" style="v" />Also Vashti <char style="add">[beautiful]</char> the Queen made a feast for the women <char style="add">[in]</char> the royal house which <char style="add">[belonged]</char> to king Achashverosh <char style="add">[I will be silent and poor]</char>.</para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="10" style="v" />On the seventh day, when the heart of the king was merry with wine, he commanded Mehuman <char style="add">[faithful]</char>, Bizta <char style="add">[booty]</char>, Harvona <char style="add">[ass-driver]</char>, Bigta <char style="add">[in the winepress]</char>, and Avagta <char style="add">[God-given]</char>, Zetar <char style="add">[star]</char>, and Karkas <char style="add">[severe]</char>, the seven chamberlains that served in the presence of Achashverosh <char style="add">[I will be silent and poor]</char> the king,</para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="11" style="v" />To bring Vashti <char style="add">[beautiful]</char> the queen before the king with the crown royal, to show the people and the princes her beauty: for she <char style="add">[was]</char> fair to look on.</para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="12" style="v" />But the queen Vashti <char style="add">[beautiful]</char> refused to come at the king’s commandment by <char style="add">[his]</char> chamberlains: therefore was the king very exceedingly angry, and his anger burned in him.</para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="13" style="v" />Then the king said to the wise men, which knew the times, (for so <char style="add">[was]</char> the king’s manner toward all that knew law and judgment:</para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="14" style="v" />And the next unto him <char style="add">[was]</char> Karshna <char style="add">[illustrious]</char>, Shetar <char style="add">[a star]</char>, Admata <char style="add">[a testimony to them]</char>, Tarshish <char style="add">[yellow jasper]</char>, Meres <char style="add">[lofty]</char>, Marsna <char style="add">[worthy]</char>, <char style="add">[and]</char> Memukhan <char style="add">[dignified]</char>, the seven princes of Paras <char style="pn">(Persia)</char> <char style="add">[pure or splendid]</char> and Madai <char style="add">[middle land]</char>, which saw the king’s face, <char style="add">[and]</char> which sat the first in the kingdom;)</para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="15" style="v" />What shall we do unto the queen Vashti <char style="add">[beautiful]</char> according to law, because she has not performed the commandment of the king Achashverosh <char style="add">[I will be silent and poor]</char> by the chamberlains?</para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="16" style="v" />And Memukhan <char style="add">[dignified]</char> answered before the king and the princes, Vashti <char style="add">[beautiful]</char> the queen has not done wrong to the king only, but also to all the princes, and to all the people that <char style="add">[are]</char> in all the provinces of the king Achashverosh <char style="add">[I will be silent and poor]</char>.</para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="17" style="v" />For <char style="add">[this]</char> deed of the queen shall come abroad unto all women, so that they shall despise their husbands in their eyes, when it shall be reported, The king Achashverosh <char style="add">[I will be silent and poor]</char> commanded Vashti <char style="add">[beautiful]</char> the queen to be brought in before him, but she came not.</para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="18" style="v" />
    <char style="add">[Likewise]</char> shall the ladies of Paras <char style="pn">(Persia)</char> <char style="add">[pure or splendid]</char> and Madai <char style="add">[middle land]</char> say this day unto all the king’s princes, which have heard of the deed of the queen. Thus <char style="add">[shall there arise]</char> too much contempt and wrath.</para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="19" style="v" />If it please the king, let there go a royal commandment from him, and let it be written among the laws of the Persians and the Medes <char style="add">[middle land]</char>, that it be not altered, That Vashti <char style="add">[beautiful]</char> come no more before king Achashverosh <char style="add">[I will be silent and poor]</char>; and let the king give her royal estate unto another that is better than she.</para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="20" style="v" />And when the king’s decree which he shall make shall be published throughout all his empire, <char style="pn">(for it is great,)</char> all the wives shall give to their husbands honour, both to great and small.</para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="21" style="v" />And the saying pleased the king and the princes; and the king did according to the word of Memukhan <char style="add">[dignified]</char>:</para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="22" style="v" />For he sent letters into all the king’s provinces, into every province according to the writing thereof, and to every people after their language, that every man should bear rule in his own house, and that <char style="add">[it]</char> should be published according to the language of every people.</para>
  <chapter number="2" style="c" />
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="1" style="v" />After these things, when the wrath of king Achashverosh <char style="add">[I will be silent and poor]</char> was appeased, he remembered Vashti <char style="add">[beautiful]</char>, and what she had done, and what was decreed against her.</para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="2" style="v" />Then said the king’s servants that ministered unto him, Let there be fair young virgins sought for the king:</para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="3" style="v" />And let the king appoint officers in all the provinces of his kingdom, that they may gather together all the fair young virgins unto Shushan <char style="add">[lily]</char> the palace, to the house of the women, unto the custody of Hegai <char style="add">[eunuch]</char> the king’s Chamberlain (eunuch or chief servant; one in charge of the king’s quarters), keeper of the women; and let their things for purification be given <char style="add">[them]</char>:</para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="4" style="v" />And let the virgin which pleases the king be queen instead of Vashti <char style="add">[beautiful]</char>. And the thing pleased the king; and he did so.</para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="5" style="v" />
    <char style="add">[Now]</char> in Shushan <char style="add">[lily]</char> the palace there was a certain Jew, whose name <char style="add">[was]</char> Mordekhai <char style="add">[little man]</char>, the son of Ya’ir <char style="add">[He Enlightens]</char>, the son of Shime’i <char style="add">[Fame]</char>, the son of Kish <char style="add">[bent]</char>, from the tribe of Binyamin <char style="add">[A Son of the Right Hand]</char>;</para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="6" style="v" />Who had been carried away from Yerushalayim <char style="add">[Foundation of Peace]</char> with the captivity which had been carried away with Y’khanyahu <char style="add">[Yehovah will establish]</char> king of Y’hudah <char style="add">[Let Him (God) Be Praised]</char>, whom N’vukhadnetzar the king of Bavel <char style="pn">(to jumble, or a confused medley of sounds; confusion; gate of the deity)</char> <char style="pn">(Babylon)</char> <char style="add">[confusion]</char> had carried away.</para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="7" style="v" />And he brought up Hadassah, that <char style="add">[is]</char>, Ester <char style="add">[star]</char>, his uncle’s daughter: for she had neither father nor mother, and the maid <char style="add">[was]</char> fair and beautiful; whom Mordekhai <char style="add">[little man]</char>, when her father and mother were dead, took for his own daughter.</para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="8" style="v" />So it came to pass, when the king’s commandment and his decree was heard, and when many maidens were gathered together unto Shushan <char style="add">[lily]</char> the palace, to the custody of Hegai, that Ester <char style="add">[star]</char> was brought also unto the king’s house, to the custody of Hegai, keeper of the women.</para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="9" style="v" />And the virgin pleased him, and she obtained kindness of him; and he speedily gave her her things for purification, with such things as belonged to her, and seven maidens, <char style="add">[which were]</char> meet to be given her, out of the king’s house: and he preferred her and her maids unto the best <char style="add">[place]</char> of the house of the women.</para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="10" style="v" />Ester <char style="add">[star]</char> had not showed her people nor her family: for Mordekhai <char style="add">[little man]</char> had charged her that she should not show <char style="add">[it]</char>.</para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="11" style="v" />And Mordekhai <char style="add">[little man]</char> walked every day before the court of the women’s house, to know how Ester <char style="add">[star]</char> did, and what should become of her.</para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="12" style="v" />Now when every maid’s turn was come to go in to king Achashverosh <char style="add">[I will be silent and poor]</char>, after that she had been twelve months, according to the manner of the women, (for so were the days of their purifications accomplished, <char style="add">[that is to say]</char>, six months with oil of myrrh, and six months with sweet odours, and with <char style="add">[other]</char> things for the purifying of the women;)</para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="13" style="v" />Then thus came <char style="add">[every]</char> virgin unto the king; whatsoever she desired was given her to go with her out of the house of the women unto the king’s house.</para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="14" style="v" />In the evening she went, and on the morrow she returned into the second house of the women, to the custody of Sha’ashgaz <char style="add">[servant of the beautiful]</char>, the king’s Chamberlain <char style="pn">(eunuch or chief servant; one in charge of the king’s quarters),</char> which kept the concubines: she came in unto the king no more, except the king delighted in her, and that she were called by name.</para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="15" style="v" />Now when the turn of Ester <char style="add">[star]</char>, the daughter of Avichayil <char style="add">[Father of Strength]</char> the uncle of Mordekhai <char style="add">[little man]</char>, who had taken her for his daughter, was come to go in unto the king, she required nothing but what Hegai the king’s Chamberlain <char style="pn">(eunuch or chief servant; one in charge of the king’s quarters)</char>, the keeper of the women, appointed. And Ester <char style="add">[star]</char> obtained favour in the sight of all them that looked upon her.</para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="16" style="v" />So Ester <char style="add">[star]</char> was taken unto king Achashverosh <char style="add">[I will be silent and poor]</char> into his house royal in the tenth month, which <char style="add">[is]</char> the month Tevet <char style="add">[goodness]</char>, in the seventh year of his reign.</para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="17" style="v" />And the king loved Ester <char style="add">[star]</char> above all the women, and she obtained grace and favour in his sight more than all the virgins; so that he set the royal crown upon her head, and made her queen instead of Vashti <char style="add">[beautiful]</char>.</para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="18" style="v" />Then the king made a great feast unto all his princes and his servants, <char style="add">[even]</char> Ester <char style="add">[star]</char>’s feast; and he made a release to the provinces, and gave gifts, according to the state of the king.</para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="19" style="v" />And when the virgins were gathered together the second time, then Mordekhai <char style="add">[little man]</char> sat in the king’s gate.</para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="20" style="v" />Ester <char style="add">[star]</char> had not <char style="add">[yet]</char> showed her family nor her people; as Mordekhai <char style="add">[little man]</char> had charged her: for Ester <char style="add">[star]</char> did the commandment of Mordekhai <char style="add">[little man]</char>, like as when she was brought up with him.</para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="21" style="v" />In those days, while Mordekhai <char style="add">[little man]</char> sat in the king’s gate, two of the king’s chamberlains, Bigtan <char style="add">[in their winepress]</char> and Teresh <char style="add">[strictness]</char>, of those which kept the door, were exceedingly angry, and sought to lay hand on the king Achashverosh <char style="add">[I will be silent and poor]</char>.</para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="22" style="v" />And the thing was known to Mordekhai <char style="add">[little man]</char>, who told <char style="add">[it]</char> unto Ester <char style="add">[star]</char> the queen; and Ester <char style="add">[star]</char> certified the king <char style="add">[thereof]</char> in Mordekhai <char style="add">[little man]</char>’s name.</para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="23" style="v" />And when inquisition was made of the matter, it was found out; therefore they were both hanged on a tree: and it was written in the book of the chronicles before the king.</para>
  <chapter number="3" style="c" />
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="1" style="v" />After these things did king Achashverosh <char style="add">[I will be silent and poor]</char> promote Haman <char style="add">[magnificent]</char> the son of Hamdata <char style="add">[double]</char> the Agagi <char style="add">[I will overtop]</char>, and advanced him, and set his seat above all the princes that <char style="add">[were]</char> with him.</para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="2" style="v" />And all the king’s servants, that <char style="add">[were]</char> in the king’s gate, bowed, and reverenced Haman <char style="add">[magnificent]</char>: for the king had so commanded concerning him. But Mordekhai <char style="add">[little man]</char> bowed not, nor did <char style="add">[him]</char> reverence.</para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="3" style="v" />Then the king’s servants, which <char style="add">[were]</char> in the king’s gate, said unto Mordekhai <char style="add">[little man]</char>, Why transgress you<char style="ord">i</char> the king’s commandment?</para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="4" style="v" />Now it came to pass, when they spoke daily unto him, and he listened not unto them, that they told Haman <char style="add">[magnificent]</char>, to see whether Mordekhai <char style="add">[little man]</char>’s matters would stand: for he had told them that he <char style="add">[was]</char> a Jew.</para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="5" style="v" />And when Haman <char style="add">[magnificent]</char> saw that Mordekhai <char style="add">[little man]</char> bowed not, nor did him reverence, then was Haman <char style="add">[magnificent]</char> full of wrath.</para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="6" style="v" />And he thought scorn to lay hands on Mordekhai <char style="add">[little man]</char> alone; for they had showed him the people of Mordekhai <char style="add">[little man]</char>: wherefore Haman <char style="add">[magnificent]</char> sought to destroy all the Y’hudim <char style="pn">(Judeans)</char> that <char style="add">[were]</char> throughout the whole kingdom of Achashverosh <char style="add">[I will be silent and poor]</char>, <char style="add">[even]</char> the people of Mordekhai <char style="add">[little man]</char>.</para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="7" style="v" />In the first month, that <char style="add">[is]</char>, the month Nisan <char style="add">[their flight]</char>, in the twelfth year of king Achashverosh <char style="add">[I will be silent and poor]</char>, they cast Pur <char style="add">[lot]</char>, that <char style="add">[is]</char>, the lot, before Haman <char style="add">[magnificent]</char> from day to day, and from month to month, <char style="add">[to]</char> the twelfth <char style="add">[month]</char>, that <char style="add">[is]</char>, the month Adar.</para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="8" style="v" />And Haman <char style="add">[magnificent]</char> said unto king Achashverosh <char style="add">[I will be silent and poor]</char>, There is a certain people scattered abroad and dispersed among the people in all the provinces of you<char style="ord">i</char> kingdom; and their laws <char style="add">[are]</char> diverse from all people; neither keep they the king’s laws: therefore it <char style="add">[is]</char> not for the king’s profit to suffer them.</para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="9" style="v" />If it please the king, let it be written that they may be destroyed: and I will pay ten thousand talents of silver to the hands of those that have the charge of the business, to bring <char style="add">[it]</char> into the king’s treasuries.</para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="10" style="v" />And the king took his ring from his hand, and gave it unto Haman <char style="add">[magnificent]</char> the son of Hamdata <char style="add">[double]</char> the Agagi <char style="add">[I will overtop]</char>, the Y’hudit <char style="pn">(Y’hudim (Judeans)’)</char> enemy.</para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="11" style="v" />And the king said unto Haman <char style="add">[magnificent]</char>, The silver <char style="add">[is]</char> given to you<char style="ord">i</char>, the people also, to do with them as it seems good to you<char style="ord">i</char>.</para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="12" style="v" />Then were the king’s scribes called on the thirteenth day of the first month, and there was written according to all that Haman <char style="add">[magnificent]</char> had commanded unto the king’s lieutenants, and to the governors that <char style="add">[were]</char> over every province, and to the rulers of every people of every province according to the writing thereof, and <char style="add">[to]</char> every people after their language; in the name of king Achashverosh <char style="add">[I will be silent and poor]</char> was it written, and sealed with the king’s ring.</para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="13" style="v" />And the letters were sent by posts into all the king’s provinces, to destroy, to kill, and to cause to perish, all Y’hudim <char style="pn">(Judeans)</char>, both young and old, little children and women, in one day, <char style="add">[even]</char> upon the thirteenth <char style="add">[day]</char> of the twelfth month, which is the month Adar, and <char style="add">[to take]</char> the spoil <char style="pn">(plunder; prey; booty)</char> of them for a prey.</para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="14" style="v" />The copy of the writing for a commandment to be given in every province was published unto all people, that they should be ready against that day.</para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="15" style="v" />The posts went out, being hastened by the king’s commandment, and the decree was given in Shushan <char style="add">[lily]</char> the palace. And the king and Haman <char style="add">[magnificent]</char> sat down to drink; but the city Shushan <char style="add">[lily]</char> was perplexed.</para>
  <chapter number="4" style="c" />
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="1" style="v" />When Mordekhai <char style="add">[little man]</char> perceived all that was done, Mordekhai <char style="add">[little man]</char> rent his clothes, and put on sackcloth with ashes, and went out into the midst of the city, and cried with a loud and a bitter cry;</para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="2" style="v" />And came even before the king’s gate: for none <char style="add">[might]</char> enter into the king’s gate clothed with sackcloth.</para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="3" style="v" />And in every province, to wherever the king’s commandment and his decree came, <char style="add">[there was]</char> great mourning among the Y’hudim <char style="pn">(Judeans)</char>, and fasting, and weeping, and wailing; and many lay in sackcloth and ashes.</para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="4" style="v" />So Ester <char style="add">[star]</char>’s maids and her chamberlains came and told <char style="add">[it]</char> her. Then was the queen exceedingly grieved; and she sent clothing to clothe Mordekhai <char style="add">[little man]</char>, and to take away his sackcloth from him: but he received <char style="add">[it]</char> not.</para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="5" style="v" />Then called Ester <char style="add">[star]</char> for Hatakh <char style="add">[truly]</char>, <char style="add">[one]</char> of the king’s chamberlains, whom he had appointed to attend upon her, and gave him a commandment to Mordekhai <char style="add">[little man]</char>, to know what it <char style="add">[was]</char>, and why it <char style="add">[was]</char>.</para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="6" style="v" />So Hatakh <char style="add">[truly]</char> went forth to Mordekhai <char style="add">[little man]</char> unto the street of the city, which <char style="add">[was]</char> before the king’s gate.</para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="7" style="v" />And Mordekhai <char style="add">[little man]</char> told him of all that had happened unto him, and of the sum of the money that Haman <char style="add">[magnificent]</char> had promised to pay to the king’s treasuries for the Y’hudim <char style="pn">(Judeans)</char>, to destroy them.</para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="8" style="v" />Also he gave him the copy of the writing of the decree that was given at Shushan <char style="add">[lily]</char> to destroy them, to show <char style="add">[it]</char> unto Ester <char style="add">[star]</char>, and to declare <char style="add">[it]</char> unto her, and to charge her that she should go in unto the king, to make supplication unto him, and to make request before him for her people.</para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="9" style="v" />And Hatakh <char style="add">[truly]</char> came and told Ester <char style="add">[star]</char> the words of Mordekhai <char style="add">[little man]</char>.</para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="10" style="v" />Again Ester <char style="add">[star]</char> spoke unto Hatakh <char style="add">[truly]</char>, and gave him commandment unto Mordekhai <char style="add">[little man]</char>;</para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="11" style="v" />All the king’s servants, and the people of the king’s provinces, do know, that whosoever, whether man or woman, shall come unto the king into the inner court, who is not called, <char style="add">[there is]</char> one law of his to put <char style="add">[him]</char> to death, except such to whom the king shall hold out the golden scepter, that he may live: but I have not been called to come in unto the king these thirty days.</para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="12" style="v" />And they told to Mordekhai <char style="add">[little man]</char> Ester <char style="add">[star]</char>’s words.</para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="13" style="v" />Then Mordekhai <char style="add">[little man]</char> commanded to answer Ester <char style="add">[star]</char>, Think not with yourself that you<char style="ord">i</char> shall escape in the king’s house, more than all the Y’hudim <char style="pn">(Judeans)</char>.</para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="14" style="v" />For if you<char style="ord">i</char> altogether hold you<char style="ord">i</char> peace at this time, <char style="add">[then]</char> shall there enlargement and deliverance arise to the Y’hudim <char style="pn">(Judeans)</char> from another place; but you<char style="ord">i</char> and you<char style="ord">i</char> father’s house shall be destroyed: and who knows whether you<char style="ord">i</char> are come to the kingdom for <char style="add">[such]</char> a time as this?</para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="15" style="v" />Then Ester <char style="add">[star]</char> bade <char style="add">[them]</char> return Mordekhai <char style="add">[little man] [this answer]</char>,</para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="16" style="v" />Go, gather together all the Y’hudim <char style="pn">(Judeans)</char> that are present in Shushan <char style="add">[lily]</char>, and fast you<char style="ord">2f</char> for me, and neither eat nor drink three days, night or day: I also and my maidens will fast likewise; and so will I go in unto the king, which <char style="add">[is]</char> not according to the law: and if I perish, I perish.</para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="17" style="v" />So Mordekhai <char style="add">[little man]</char> went his way, and did according to all that Ester <char style="add">[star]</char> had commanded him.</para>
  <chapter number="5" style="c" />
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="1" style="v" />Now it came to pass on the third day, that Ester <char style="add">[star]</char> put on <char style="add">[her]</char> royal <char style="add">[apparel]</char>, and stood in the inner court of the king’s house, over against the king’s house: and the king sat upon his royal throne in the royal house, over against the gate of the house.</para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="2" style="v" />And it was so, when the king saw Ester <char style="add">[star]</char> the queen standing in the court, <char style="add">[that]</char> she obtained favour in his sight: and the king held out to Ester <char style="add">[star]</char> the golden scepter that <char style="add">[was]</char> in his hand. So Ester <char style="add">[star]</char> drew near, and touched the top of the scepter.</para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="3" style="v" />Then said the king unto her, What will you<char style="ord">i</char>, queen Ester <char style="add">[star]</char>? and what <char style="add">[is]</char> you<char style="ord">i</char> request? it shall be even given you<char style="ord">i</char> to the half of the kingdom.</para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="4" style="v" />And Ester <char style="add">[star]</char> answered, If <char style="add">[it seem]</char> good unto the king, let the king and Haman <char style="add">[magnificent]</char> come this day unto the banquet that I have prepared for him.</para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="5" style="v" />Then the king said, Cause Haman <char style="add">[magnificent]</char> to make haste, that he may do as Ester <char style="add">[star]</char> has said. So the king and Haman <char style="add">[magnificent]</char> came to the banquet that Ester <char style="add">[star]</char> had prepared.</para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="6" style="v" />And the king said unto Ester <char style="add">[star]</char> at the banquet of wine, What <char style="add">[is]</char> you<char style="ord">i</char> petition? and it shall be granted you<char style="ord">i</char>: and what <char style="add">[is]</char> you<char style="ord">i</char> request? even to the half of the kingdom it shall be performed.</para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="7" style="v" />Then answered Ester <char style="add">[star]</char>, and said, My petition and my request <char style="add">[is]</char>;</para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="8" style="v" />If I have found favour in the sight of the king, and if it please the king to grant my petition, and to perform my request, let the king and Haman <char style="add">[magnificent]</char> come to the banquet that I shall prepare for them, and I will do tomorrow as the king has said.</para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="9" style="v" />Then went Haman <char style="add">[magnificent]</char> forth that day joyful and with a glad heart: but when Haman <char style="add">[magnificent]</char> saw Mordekhai <char style="add">[little man]</char> in the king’s gate, that he stood not up, nor moved for him, he was full of indignation against Mordekhai <char style="add">[little man]</char>.</para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="10" style="v" />Nevertheless Haman <char style="add">[magnificent]</char> refrained himself: and when he came home, he sent and called for his friends, and Zeresh <char style="add">[gold]</char> his wife.</para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="11" style="v" />And Haman <char style="add">[magnificent]</char> told them of the glory of his riches, and the multitude of his children, and all <char style="add">[the things]</char> wherein the king had promoted him, and how he had advanced him above the princes and servants of the king.</para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="12" style="v" />Haman <char style="add">[magnificent]</char> said moreover, Yes, Ester <char style="add">[star]</char> the queen did let no man come in with the king unto the banquet that she had prepared but myself; and tomorrow am I invited unto her also with the king.</para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="13" style="v" />Yet all this avails me nothing, so long as I see Mordekhai <char style="add">[little man]</char> the Jew sitting at the king’s gate.</para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="14" style="v" />Then said Zeresh <char style="add">[gold]</char> his wife and all his friends unto him, Let a gallow (to approve, commend, accept)s be made of fifty cubits high, and tomorrow speak you<char style="ord">i</char> unto the king that Mordekhai <char style="add">[little man]</char> may be hanged thereon: then go you<char style="ord">i</char> in merrily with the king unto the banquet. And the thing pleased Haman <char style="add">[magnificent]</char>; and he caused the gallow <char style="pn">(to approve, commend, accept)</char>s to be made.</para>
  <chapter number="6" style="c" />
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="1" style="v" />On that night could not the king sleep, and he commanded to bring the book of records of the chronicles; and they were read before the king.</para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="2" style="v" />And it was found written, that Mordekhai <char style="add">[little man]</char> had told of Bigtana <char style="add">[in their winepress]</char> and Teresh <char style="add">[strictness]</char>, two of the king’s chamberlains, the keepers of the door, who sought to lay hand on the king Achashverosh <char style="add">[I will be silent and poor]</char>.</para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="3" style="v" />And the king said, What honour and dignity has been done to Mordekhai <char style="add">[little man]</char> for this? Then said the king’s servants that ministered unto him, There is nothing done for him.</para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="4" style="v" />And the king said, Who <char style="add">[is]</char> in the court? Now Haman <char style="add">[magnificent]</char> was come into the outward court of the king’s house, to speak unto the king to hang Mordekhai <char style="add">[little man]</char> on the gallow <char style="add">(to approve, commend, accept)</char>s that he had prepared for him.</para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="5" style="v" />And the king’s servants said unto him, Behold, Haman <char style="add">[magnificent]</char> stands in the court. And the king said, Let him come in.</para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="6" style="v" />So Haman <char style="add">[magnificent]</char> came in. And the king said unto him, What shall be done unto the man whom the king delights to honour? Now Haman <char style="add">[magnificent]</char> thought in his heart, To whom would the king delight to do honour more than to myself?</para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="7" style="v" />And Haman <char style="add">[magnificent]</char> answered the king, For the man whom the king delights to honour,</para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="8" style="v" />Let the royal apparel be brought which the king <char style="add">[uses]</char> to wear, and the horse that the king rides upon, and the crown royal which is set upon his head:</para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="9" style="v" />And let this apparel and horse be delivered to the hand of one of the king’s most noble princes, that they may array <char style="pn">(to put on; to clothe)</char> the man <char style="add">[withal]</char> whom the king delights to honour, and bring him on horseback through the street of the city, and proclaim before him, Thus shall it be done to the man whom the king delights to honour.</para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="10" style="v" />Then the king said to Haman <char style="add">[magnificent]</char>, Make haste, <char style="add">[and]</char> take the apparel and the horse, as you<char style="ord">i</char> have said, and do even so to Mordekhai <char style="add">[little man]</char> the Jew, that sits at the king’s gate: let nothing fail of all that you<char style="ord">i</char> have spoken.</para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="11" style="v" />Then took Haman <char style="add">[magnificent]</char> the apparel and the horse, and arrayed Mordekhai <char style="add">[little man]</char>, and brought him on horseback through the street of the city, and proclaimed before him, Thus shall it be done unto the man whom the king delights to honour.</para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="12" style="v" />And Mordekhai <char style="add">[little man]</char> came again to the king’s gate. But Haman <char style="add">[magnificent]</char> hasted to his house mourning, and having his head covered.</para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="13" style="v" />And Haman <char style="add">[magnificent]</char> told Zeresh <char style="add">[gold]</char> his wife and all his friends every <char style="add">[thing]</char> that had befallen him. Then said his wise men and Zeresh <char style="add">[gold]</char> his wife unto him, If Mordekhai <char style="add">[little man] [be]</char> of the seed of the Y’hudim <char style="pn">(Judeans)</char>, before whom you<char style="ord">i</char> have begun to fall, you<char style="ord">i</char> shall not prevail against him, but shall surely fall before him.</para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="14" style="v" />And while they <char style="add">[were]</char> yet talking with him, came the king’s chamberlains, and hasted to bring Haman <char style="add">[magnificent]</char> unto the banquet that Ester <char style="add">[star]</char> had prepared.</para>
  <chapter number="7" style="c" />
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="1" style="v" />So the king and Haman <char style="add">[magnificent]</char> came to banquet with Ester <char style="add">[star]</char> the queen.</para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="2" style="v" />And the king said again unto Ester <char style="add">[star]</char> on the second day at the banquet of wine, What <char style="add">[is]</char> you<char style="ord">i</char> petition, queen Ester <char style="add">[star]</char>? and it shall be granted you<char style="ord">i</char>: and what <char style="add">[is]</char> you<char style="ord">i</char> request? and it shall be performed, <char style="add">[even]</char> to the half of the kingdom.</para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="3" style="v" />Then Ester <char style="add">[star]</char> the queen answered and said, If I have found favour in you<char style="ord">i</char> sight, O king, and if it please the king, let my life be given me at my petition, and my people at my request:</para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="4" style="v" />For we are sold, I and my people, to be destroyed, to be slain, and to perish. But if we had been sold for bondmen and bondwomen, I had held my tongue, although the enemy could not countervail <char style="pn">(be or make equal; compensate)</char> the king’s damage.</para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="5" style="v" />Then the king Achashverosh <char style="add">[I will be silent and poor]</char> answered and said unto Ester <char style="add">[star]</char> the queen, Who is he, and where is he, that dared <char style="pn">(was bold enough)</char> presume in his heart to do so?</para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="6" style="v" />And Ester <char style="add">[star]</char> said, The adversary and enemy <char style="add">[is]</char> this wicked Haman <char style="add">[magnificent]</char>. Then Haman <char style="add">[magnificent]</char> was afraid before the king and the queen.</para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="7" style="v" />And the king arising from the banquet of wine in his wrath <char style="add">[went]</char> into the palace garden: and Haman <char style="add">[magnificent]</char> stood up to make request for his life to Ester <char style="add">[star]</char> the queen; for he saw that there was evil determined against him by the king.</para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="8" style="v" />Then the king returned out of the palace garden into the place of the banquet of wine; and Haman <char style="add">[magnificent]</char> was fallen upon the bed whereon Ester <char style="add">[star] [was]</char>. Then said the king, Will he force the queen also before me in the house? As the word went out of the king’s mouth, they covered Haman <char style="add">[magnificent]</char>’s face.</para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="9" style="v" />And Harvonah <char style="add">[ass-driver]</char>, one of the chamberlains, said before the king, Behold also, the gallow <char style="pn">(to approve, commend, accept)</char>s fifty cubits high, which Haman <char style="add">[magnificent]</char> had made for Mordekhai <char style="add">[little man]</char>, who had spoken good for the king, stands in the house of Haman <char style="add">[magnificent]</char>. Then the king said, Hang him thereon.</para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="10" style="v" />So they hanged Haman <char style="add">[magnificent]</char> on the gallow <char style="pn">(to approve, commend, accept)</char>s that he had prepared for Mordekhai <char style="add">[little man]</char>. Then was the king’s wrath pacified.</para>
  <chapter number="8" style="c" />
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="1" style="v" />On that day did the king Achashverosh <char style="add">[I will be silent and poor]</char> give the house of Haman <char style="add">[magnificent]</char> the Y’hudit <char style="pn">(Y’hudim (Judeans)’)</char> enemy unto Ester <char style="add">[star]</char> the queen. And Mordekhai <char style="add">[little man]</char> came before the king; for Ester <char style="add">[star]</char> had told what he <char style="add">[was]</char> unto her.</para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="2" style="v" />And the king took off his ring, which he had taken from Haman <char style="add">[magnificent]</char>, and gave it unto Mordekhai <char style="add">[little man]</char>. And Ester <char style="add">[star]</char> set Mordekhai <char style="add">[little man]</char> over the house of Haman <char style="add">[magnificent]</char>.</para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="3" style="v" />And Ester <char style="add">[star]</char> spoke yet again before the king, and fell down at his feet, and besought him with tears to put away the mischief of Haman <char style="add">[magnificent]</char> the Agagi <char style="add">[I will overtop]</char>, and his device that he had devised against the Y’hudim <char style="pn">(Judeans)</char>.</para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="4" style="v" />Then the king held out the golden scepter toward Ester <char style="add">[star]</char>. So Ester <char style="add">[star]</char> arose, and stood before the king,</para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="5" style="v" />And said, If it please the king, and if I have found favour in his sight, and the thing <char style="add">[seem]</char> right before the king, and I <char style="add">[be]</char> pleasing in his eyes, let it be written to reverse the letters devised by Haman <char style="add">[magnificent]</char> the son of Hamdata <char style="add">[double]</char> the Agagi <char style="add">[I will overtop]</char>, which he wrote to destroy the Y’hudim <char style="pn">(Judeans)</char> which <char style="add">[are]</char> in all the king’s provinces:</para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="6" style="v" />For how can I endure to see the evil that shall come unto my people? or how can I endure to see the destruction of my family?</para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="7" style="v" />Then the king Achashverosh <char style="add">[I will be silent and poor]</char> said unto Ester <char style="add">[star]</char> the queen and to Mordekhai <char style="add">[little man]</char> the Jew, Behold, I have given Ester <char style="add">[star]</char> the house of Haman <char style="add">[magnificent]</char>, and him they have hanged upon the gallow <char style="pn">(to approve, commend, accept)</char>s, because he laid his hand upon the Y’hudim <char style="pn">(Judeans)</char>.</para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="8" style="v" />Write you<char style="ord">2f</char> also for the Y’hudim <char style="pn">(Judeans)</char>, as it likes you, in the king’s name, and seal <char style="add">[it]</char> with the king’s ring: for the writing which is written in the king’s name, and sealed with the king’s ring, may no man reverse.</para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="9" style="v" />Then were the king’s scribes called at that time in the third month, that <char style="add">[is]</char>, the month Sivan, on the three and twentieth <char style="add">[day]</char> thereof; and it was written according to all that Mordekhai <char style="add">[little man]</char> commanded unto the Y’hudim <char style="pn">(Judeans)</char>, and to the lieutenants, and the deputies and rulers of the provinces which <char style="add">[are]</char> from India unto Ethiopia <char style="add">[Kush]</char>, an hundred twenty and seven provinces, unto every province according to the writing thereof, and unto every people after their language, and to the Y’hudim <char style="pn">(Judeans)</char> according to their writing, and according to their language.</para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="10" style="v" />And he wrote in the king Achashverosh <char style="add">[I will be silent and poor]</char>’ name, and sealed <char style="add">[it]</char> with the king’s ring, and sent letters by posts on horseback, <char style="add">[and]</char> riders on mules, camels, <char style="add">[and]</char> young dromedaries:</para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="11" style="v" />Wherein the king granted the Y’hudim <char style="pn">(Judeans)</char> which <char style="add">[were]</char> in every city to gather themselves together, and to stand for their life, to destroy, to slay, and to cause to perish, all the power of the people and province that would assault them, <char style="add">[both]</char> little ones and women, and <char style="add">[to take]</char> the spoil <char style="pn">(plunder; prey; booty)</char> of them for a prey,</para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="12" style="v" />Upon one day in all the provinces of king Achashverosh <char style="add">[I will be silent and poor]</char>, <char style="add">[namely]</char>, upon the thirteenth <char style="add">[day]</char> of the twelfth month, which <char style="add">[is]</char> the month Adar.</para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="13" style="v" />The copy of the writing for a commandment to be given in every province <char style="add">[was]</char> published unto all people, and that the Y’hudim <char style="pn">(Judeans)</char> should be ready against that day to avenge themselves on their enemies.</para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="14" style="v" />
    <char style="add">[So]</char> the posts that rode upon mules <char style="add">[and]</char> camels went out, being hastened and pressed on by the king’s commandment. And the decree was given at Shushan <char style="add">[lily]</char> the palace.</para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="15" style="v" />And Mordekhai <char style="add">[little man]</char> went out from the presence of the king in royal apparel of blue and white, and with a great crown of gold, and with a garment of fine linen and purple: and the city of Shushan <char style="add">[lily]</char> rejoiced and was glad.</para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="16" style="v" />The Y’hudim <char style="pn">(Judeans)</char> had light, and gladness, and joy, and honour.</para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="17" style="v" />And in every province, and in every city, to wherever the king’s commandment and his decree came, the Y’hudim <char style="pn">(Judeans)</char> had joy and gladness, a feast and a good day. And many of the people of the land became Y’hudim <char style="pn">(Judeans)</char>; for the fear of the Y’hudim <char style="pn">(Judeans)</char> fell upon them.</para>
  <chapter number="9" style="c" />
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="1" style="v" />Now in the twelfth month, that <char style="add">[is]</char>, the month Adar, on the thirteenth day of the same, when the king’s commandment and his decree drew near to be put in execution, in the day that the enemies of the Y’hudim <char style="pn">(Judeans)</char> hoped to have power over them, <char style="pn">(though it was turned to the contrary, that the Y’hudim (Judeans) had rule over them that hated them;)</char></para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="2" style="v" />The Y’hudim <char style="pn">(Judeans)</char> gathered themselves together in their cities throughout all the provinces of the king Achashverosh <char style="add">[I will be silent and poor]</char>, to lay hand on such as sought their hurt: and no man could withstand them; for the fear of them fell upon all people.</para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="3" style="v" />And all the rulers of the provinces, and the lieutenants, and the deputies, and officers of the king, helped the Y’hudim <char style="pn">(Judeans)</char>; because the fear of Mordekhai <char style="add">[little man]</char> fell upon them.</para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="4" style="v" />For Mordekhai <char style="add">[little man] [was]</char> great in the king’s house, and his fame went out throughout all the provinces: for this man Mordekhai <char style="add">[little man]</char> waxed greater and greater.</para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="5" style="v" />Thus the Y’hudim <char style="pn">(Judeans)</char> struck all their enemies with the stroke of the sword, and slaughter, and destruction, and did what they would unto those that hated them.</para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="6" style="v" />And in Shushan <char style="add">[lily]</char> the palace the Y’hudim <char style="pn">(Judeans)</char> killed and destroyed five hundred men.</para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="7" style="v" />And Parshandata <char style="add">[given by prayer]</char>, and Dalfon <char style="add">[dripping]</char>, and Aspata <char style="add">[the enticed gathered]</char>,</para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="8" style="v" />And Porata <char style="add">[fruitfulness, or frustration]</char>, and Adalya <char style="add">[I shall be drawn up of Yah]</char>, and Aridata <char style="add">[the lion of the decree]</char>,</para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="9" style="v" />And Parmashta <char style="add">[superior]</char>, and Arisai <char style="add">[meaning disputed]</char>, and Aridai <char style="add">[the lion is enough]</char>, and Vaizata <char style="add">[strong as the wind]</char>,</para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="10" style="v" />The ten sons of Haman <char style="add">[magnificent]</char> the son of Hamdata <char style="add">[double]</char>, the enemy of the Y’hudim <char style="pn">(Judeans)</char>, killed they; but on the spoil <char style="pn">(plunder; prey; booty)</char> laid they not their hand.</para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="11" style="v" />On that day the number of those that were slain in Shushan <char style="add">[lily]</char> the palace was brought before the king.</para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="12" style="v" />And the king said unto Ester <char style="add">[star]</char> the queen, The Y’hudim <char style="pn">(Judeans)</char> have slain and destroyed five hundred men in Shushan <char style="add">[lily]</char> the palace, and the ten sons of Haman <char style="add">[magnificent]</char>; what have they done in the rest of the king’s provinces? now what <char style="add">[is]</char> you<char style="ord">i</char> petition? and it shall be granted you<char style="ord">i</char>: or what <char style="add">[is]</char> you<char style="ord">i</char> request further? and it shall be done.</para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="13" style="v" />Then said Ester <char style="add">[star]</char>, If it please the king, let it be granted to the Y’hudim <char style="pn">(Judeans)</char> which <char style="add">[are]</char> in Shushan <char style="add">[lily]</char> to do tomorrow also according unto this day’s decree, and let Haman <char style="add">[magnificent]</char>’s ten sons be hanged upon the gallow (to approve, commend, accept)s.</para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="14" style="v" />And the king commanded it so to be done: and the decree was given at Shushan <char style="add">[lily]</char>; and they hanged Haman <char style="add">[magnificent]</char>’s ten sons.</para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="15" style="v" />For the Y’hudim <char style="pn">(Judeans)</char> that <char style="add">[were]</char> in Shushan <char style="add">[lily]</char> gathered themselves together on the fourteenth day also of the month Adar, and killed three hundred men at Shushan <char style="add">[lily]</char>; but on the prey they laid not their hand.</para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="16" style="v" />But the other Y’hudim <char style="pn">(Judeans)</char> that <char style="add">[were]</char> in the king’s provinces gathered themselves together, and stood for their lives, and had rest from their enemies, and killed of their foes seventy and five thousand, but they laid not their hands on the prey,</para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="17" style="v" />On the thirteenth day of the month Adar; and on the fourteenth day of the same rested they, and made it a day of feasting and gladness.</para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="18" style="v" />But the Y’hudim <char style="pn">(Judeans)</char> that <char style="add">[were]</char> at Shushan <char style="add">[lily]</char> assembled together on the thirteenth <char style="add">[day]</char> thereof, and on the fourteenth thereof; and on the fifteenth <char style="add">[day]</char> of the same they rested, and made it a day of feasting and gladness.</para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="19" style="v" />Therefore the Y’hudim <char style="pn">(Judeans)</char> of the villages, that dwelt in the unwalled towns, made the fourteenth day of the month Adar <char style="add">[a day of]</char> gladness and feasting, and a good day, and of sending portions one to another.</para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="20" style="v" />And Mordekhai <char style="add">[little man]</char> wrote these things, and sent letters unto all the Y’hudim <char style="pn">(Judeans)</char> that <char style="add">[were]</char> in all the provinces of the king Achashverosh <char style="add">[I will be silent and poor]</char>, <char style="add">[both]</char> near and far,</para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="21" style="v" />To establish <char style="add">[this]</char> among them, that they should keep the fourteenth day of the month Adar, and the fifteenth day of the same, yearly,</para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="22" style="v" />As the days wherein the Y’hudim <char style="pn">(Judeans)</char> rested from their enemies, and the month which was turned unto them from sorrow to joy, and from mourning into a good day: that they should make them days of feasting and joy, and of sending portions one to another, and gifts to the poor.</para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="23" style="v" />And the Y’hudim <char style="pn">(Judeans)</char> undertook to do as they had begun, and as Mordekhai <char style="add">[little man]</char> had written unto them;</para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="24" style="v" />Because Haman <char style="add">[magnificent]</char> the son of Hamdata <char style="add">[double]</char>, the Agagi <char style="add">[I will overtop]</char>, the enemy of all the Y’hudim <char style="pn">(Judeans)</char>, had devised against the Y’hudim <char style="pn">(Judeans)</char> to destroy them, and had cast Pur <char style="add">[lot]</char>, that <char style="add">[is]</char>, the lot, to consume them, and to destroy them;</para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="25" style="v" />But when [Ester <char style="add">[star]</char>] came before the king, he commanded by letters that his wicked device, which he devised against the Y’hudim <char style="pn">(Judeans)</char>, should return upon his own head, and that he and his sons should be hanged on the gallow <char style="pn">(to approve, commend, accept)</char>s.</para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="26" style="v" />For what reason they called these days Purim after the name of Pur <char style="add">[lot]</char>. Therefore for all the words of this letter, and <char style="add">[of that]</char> which they had seen concerning this matter, and which had come unto them,</para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="27" style="v" />The Y’hudim <char style="pn">(Judeans)</char> ordained, and took upon them, and upon their seed, and upon all such as joined themselves unto them, so as it should not fail, that they would keep these two days according to their writing, and according to their <char style="add">[appointed]</char> time every year;</para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="28" style="v" />And <char style="add">[that]</char> these days <char style="add">[should be]</char> remembered and kept throughout every generation, every family, every province, and every city; and <char style="add">[that]</char> these days of Purim should not fail from among the Y’hudim <char style="pn">(Judeans)</char>, nor the memorial of them perish from their seed.</para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="29" style="v" />Then Ester <char style="add">[star]</char> the queen, the daughter of Avichayil <char style="add">[Father of Strength]</char>, and Mordekhai <char style="add">[little man]</char> the Jew, wrote with all authority, to confirm this second letter of Purim.</para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="30" style="v" />And he sent the letters unto all the Y’hudim <char style="pn">(Judeans)</char>, to the hundred twenty and seven provinces of the kingdom of Achashverosh <char style="add">[I will be silent and poor]</char>, <char style="add">[with]</char> words of peace and truth,</para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="31" style="v" />To confirm these days of Purim in their times <char style="add">[appointed]</char>, according as Mordekhai <char style="add">[little man]</char> the Jew and Ester <char style="add">[star]</char> the queen had enjoined them, and as they had decreed for themselves and for their seed, the matters of the fastings and their cry.</para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="32" style="v" />And the decree of Ester <char style="add">[star]</char> confirmed these matters of Purim; and it was written in the book.</para>
  <chapter number="10" style="c" />
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="1" style="v" />And the king Achashverosh <char style="add">[I will be silent and poor]</char> laid a tribute upon the land, and <char style="add">[upon]</char> the isles of the sea.</para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="2" style="v" />And all the acts of his power and of his might, and the declaration of the greatness of Mordekhai <char style="add">[little man]</char>, whereunto the king advanced him, <char style="add">[are]</char> they not written in the book of the Chronicles of the Kings of Madai <char style="add">[middle land]</char> and Paras <char style="pn">(Persia)</char> <char style="add">[pure or splendid]</char>?</para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="3" style="v" />For Mordekhai <char style="add">[little man]</char> the Jew <char style="add">[was]</char> next unto king Achashverosh <char style="add">[I will be silent and poor]</char>, and great among the Y’hudim <char style="pn">(Judeans)</char>, and accepted of the multitude of his brethren, seeking the wealth of his people, and speaking peace to all his seed.</para>
</usx>